```grf:context id="starter-guide"
{
  "format": "grf.public-document/v1",
  "title": "Quickstart",
  "content": "GRF downstream starter ? 0.3.0-rc2\n\nFree MIT client tooling. Python 3.11+; standard library only. The private evaluator is not included.\n\nVerify the extracted release:\n    python verify_release.py\n    python -m unittest test_client test_starter test_process -v\n\nCreate a project in a new directory (existing directories are refused):\n    python grf_starter.py init my-project --intent \"Build and verify my application\"\n    cd my-project\n    python grf.py session sign-in --task \"Implement and test the first application behavior\"\n    python grf.py check\n\nThe last command MUST fail initially. Replace checks/acceptance.py with real assertions against your application. Edit project.grf to define intent, scope, and named checks. Add checks for your language/toolchain using argv arrays; {python} selects the current interpreter. No shell expansion is performed. A zero exit code alone cannot prove a test is meaningful.\n\nResume after a disconnect:\n    python grf.py session status\n    python grf.py session resume\n\nFinish only after actual implementation and passing checks:\n    python grf.py check\n    python grf.py session complete --evidence result.json --summary \"Describe the verified project outcome\"\n    python grf.py session sign-out\n\nSupply a real, nonempty project evidence file; result.json is a placeholder name, not generated proof. Repeat --evidence for additional files. Receipts and session state remain local in .grf/. Sign-out re-runs checks and rejects changed evidence. Keep a backup of that local state if you need it across machines. A writer.lock left after a process crash must only be removed after confirming its writer is no longer running.\n\nGenerated files:\n- project.grf: project intent, scope, check commands, and admission mode.\n- AGENTS.grf: explicit agent entry instructions. Ask your agent to read it; agents that only auto-load AGENTS.md need an explicit entry instruction.\n- grf.py and .grf-runtime/: project-local runner with integrity pins; no PATH runtime fallback.\n- checks/acceptance.py: deliberately failing acceptance placeholder.\n- .grf/: durable session, event history, and counted receipts (gitignored).\n\nAPI mode (optional):\nLocal mode is useful without a service or account. Remote mode uses HTTPS mutual TLS, never customer SSH. Obtain the verified API hostname, server CA, unique client certificate/key, project and policy from the operator. Keep them under credentials/, outside Git. Copy enrollment.example.json to credentials/enrollment.json and supply issued values. Change admission.mode in project.grf to remote. Missing credentials, unavailable API, or a denied response prevent launch; there is no local fallback.\n\nEach remote check sends only its exact action digest, project and policy. The action includes resolved argv, absolute project directory, check ID as scope, and session ID as identity. Operator approval must match this exact tuple; session changes require corresponding action approval. This release does not provide automatic public enrollment or a full remote GRF evaluator.\n\nBoundaries:\nThis is a cooperative local workflow runner, not an OS sandbox. Project writers can change code, pins, evidence and checks. Scope text is guidance; it is not executable policy. The runner cleans up its Windows job or POSIX process group on normal exit, timeout and interruption. This covers cooperative descendants, not POSIX processes deliberately escaping the group or work launched through external services. Children share the initial approval. Checks cannot leave intentional background daemons running in that job/group. Local output is retained in receipts; do not run checks that print secrets. Do not claim full evaluator equivalence, universal enforcement, or production readiness from these tests.\n\nUpgrade by creating a fresh starter from a verified release and reviewing the changes before deliberately updating a project's pinned runtime. Do not overwrite active session state. Uninstall by archiving evidence and removing only the project you intend to retire. Revoke any remote enrollment separately.\n"
}
```
